James asked whether it's possible to construct a regression model's \(R^2\) statistic from its log likelihood and the number of observations. I clarified that \(R^2\) cannot be derived solely from the log likelihood and \(n\) because it relies on the residual variation relative to total variation. For Gaussian OLS models with an intercept, if the log-likelihood of the intercept-only (null) model is available, \(R^2\) can be calculated using a specific formula involving the likelihoods of both the full and null models. I also explained that while the likelihood difference helps identify \(\mathrm{SSE}/\mathrm{SST}\), this method does not apply to REML likelihoods, non-Gaussian models, or weighted regressions.
